---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';

const snapshot = [
  { label: 'Organization', value: 'Dental practice with 3 locations in a bilingual Italian-German territory, Northern Italy' },
  { label: 'Timeframe', value: 'Advertising management since 2025' },
  { label: 'Role', value: 'Google Ads and Meta Ads strategy and management, copywriting, bilingual communication setup' },
  { label: 'Channels', value: 'Google Ads Search and Local (primary), Meta Ads' },
  { label: 'Team', value: 'Practice owners (German adaptation), social media manager' },
];

const results = [
  { metric: '−53%', label: 'cost per lead, same format 2025 → 2026' },
  { metric: '4×', label: 'bilingual structure efficiency vs monolingual' },
  { metric: '+40%', label: 'revenue 2024 → 2026, figure shared by the practice' },
];
---
<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Local marketing in a bilingual territory: three locations, two languages, one system"
  description="Case study: bilingual Italian-German advertising for a multi-location practice. Google Ads Search and Local, Meta Ads, cost per lead halved in a year."
  eyebrow="Google Ads · Meta Ads · Bilingual communication"
  snapshot={snapshot}
  results={results}
>
  <h2>The context</h2>
  <p>
    Three locations spread across an area where language isn't a detail but a market factor: part
    of the catchment searches for "dentista", the other for "Zahnarzt". The practice communicates
    officially in both languages, with dedicated email addresses and bilingual staff. The three
    locations didn't start from the same position, though: one in particular struggled far more
    than the other two to generate inquiries.
  </p>

  <h2>The challenge</h2>
  <p>
    Building an advertising presence that works in two languages without doubling costs and
    without diluting the message — and rebalancing demand across locations with very different
    potential.
  </p>

  <h2>My role</h2>
  <p>
    Strategy and direct management of both channels since 2025. I wrote the Italian campaign copy
    and defined the strategic framework behind it; the German copy came out of a collaboration
    with the practice owners, to whom I explained the logic behind the Italian versions so the
    German rendering would be a cultural adaptation, not a literal translation. It's a deliberate
    methodological choice: to a native-speaking audience, mechanically translated copy is
    recognizable at first glance.
  </p>

  <h2>Analysis and insight</h2>
  <p>
    The underlying question was whether it made sense to split the two audiences into separate
    campaign structures or keep them together. The data answered clearly: the bilingual structure
    produced conversions at a cost roughly four times lower than the campaign dedicated solely to
    the German-speaking audience.
  </p>
  <p>
    I therefore chose to consolidate everything into a single structure, pausing the separate one.
    The reason wasn't just cost: running two campaigns competing over the same territory scattered
    searches and made results harder to read. Unifying made performance more stable and easier to
    optimize.
  </p>
  <p>
    Worth noting: here "conversion" doesn't just mean a submitted form — it covers the full set of
    local actions that actually matter for a multi-location practice: calls, website inquiries,
    direction requests, WhatsApp contacts.
  </p>

  <h2>Execution</h2>
  <p>
    On Google Ads: bilingual Search coverage across the three locations, paired with local
    campaigns to capture proximity search. On Meta: lead generation campaigns tied to recurring
    initiatives — an implantology open week, prevention month, hygiene and whitening — with
    creative and targeting differentiated by location, plus low-cost amplification of organic
    content (CPC between €0.09 and €0.32) to maintain continuous presence between one initiative
    and the next.
  </p>
  <p>
    For the underperforming location we worked on two fronts. Together with the social media
    manager we built a dedicated storytelling project that highlighted the specialists at that
    location, the quality of care and the human side of the team. In parallel I ran dedicated Meta
    lead generation campaigns, focused on the treatments with the best ratio between actual demand
    and margin — instead of pushing a generic offer on a catchment that was already responding
    poorly.
  </p>
  <p class="open-point">
    A detail that says more than many results: the campaign for that location reached its third
    version before finding the right configuration. The work is iterative, not linear.
  </p>

  <h2>Results</h2>
  <p>
    The most solid figure is the improvement with the same format: the same implantology lead
    generation campaign went from a cost per lead of €40.44 (May 2025) to €19.15 (April 2026) —
    roughly half — on a lead volume that nearly doubled, from 18 to 30. Overall the recurring
    initiatives generated 58 leads on Meta.
  </p>
  <p>
    On the business side, the figures the practice shared during periodic reviews indicate patient
    base growth of over 30% and revenue growth of over 40% between 2024 and 2026, with a
    significant recovery in inquiries for the location that started furthest behind.
  </p>
  <p class="open-point">
    A note on the source: unlike other projects, this client didn't have formal tracking all the
    way through to revenue. The growth figures come from periodic reviews with the owners, not
    from a structured measurement system: I report them as order-of-magnitude indications, not as
    verified metrics.
  </p>

  <h2>What I learned</h2>
  <p>
    That bilingualism isn't a translation problem but a campaign architecture one: keeping the two
    audiences together turned out to be more efficient than separating them, against the initial
    intuition. And that when locations have different potential, the aggregate figure hides more
    than it reveals — it's only by drilling down to the single location that you understand where
    to act, and with which lever.
  </p>
</CaseStudyLayout>
